INT. GRAIN ELEVATOR - ROLLER DERBY TRACK - NIGHT

A concrete silo floor, converted. Oval track taped on the slab, plywood bleachers, one hanging bulb per corner. Dust floats in the beams.

The Harvest Hellcats are down nine in the final jam. Their jammer, MARISOL "BRUISE" CASTILLO (20s, shaved sides, mouthguard dangling), sits on the infield bench lacing her skates tight enough to hurt.

Beside her, team captain TAMARA GRAY-OYELARAN (30s, tall, eye-black, taped wrist) watches the clock. Across the track, rival jammer DOT KWIATKOWSKI (40s, braids, quiet) stretches her calf, never looking over.

Referee JONAS FELD (50s, striped shirt, whistle in his teeth) walks the track, checking the lines.

Tamara watches Bruise. Bruise watches the floor.

The clock reads 1:14.

TAMARA
Left side. Don't force it.

BRUISE
I wasn't going to.

TAMARA
You were.

BRUISE
I'm fine.

Tamara slaps the bench between them. Once. Bruise nods without lifting her head.

Jonas blows a short whistle. Skaters line up. Bruise stands. Dot stands. Neither looks at the other.

JONAS
Jammers to the line.

TAMARA
(to Bruise)
Four points a lap. That's all.

BRUISE
I know.

TAMARA
Then go.

Bruise rolls to the starting line.

DOT
(to Bruise)
You're limping.

BRUISE
(to Dot)
You're old.

DOT
(to Bruise)
I'm patient.

BRUISE
(to Dot)
You're slow.

Jonas raises his whistle.

TAMARA
Do not watch her feet.

BRUISE
Whose feet?

DOT
Mine.

Beat. The grain-elevator echo carries it.

JONAS
Quiet on the line.

TAMARA
It's a three-lap jam, Bruise.

BRUISE
I said I know.

DOT
Give her a minute. She's praying.

BRUISE
I'm not praying.

DOT
You're moving your lips.

BRUISE
I'm counting.

TAMARA
She counts like her mother.

BRUISE
Leave my mother out of it.

BRUISE
Just blow it already.

DOT ^
Just blow it already.

JONAS
Last warning. Hush.

Bruise rolls her shoulders. Dot rolls hers. Their eyes finally meet. Two seconds. Three.

Jonas lifts the whistle. A cold quiet drops over the whole silo.

The whistle shrieks.
